? A mental model is:
+ An internal simulation the user uses to predict how a system behaves
- The system's source code and internal data structures
- An architecture diagram drawn by the designer for developers
- The set of passwords and settings a user remembers
= Mismatched models cause errors.

? The bus bell rope was silently replaced by seat buttons with no signs. Passengers are confused because:
+ Their old mental model no longer applies, and there are no signifiers
- The new buttons are mechanically broken and don't ring the bell
- Passengers have poor memory and forget where the rope was
- The bus moves too fast for passengers to reach the buttons
= If the rope were simply broken, the model would be correct but the mechanism faulty.

? An interface representation should match:
+ The user's mental model, not the implementation model
- The database schema that is used to store the data
- The programmer's code structure, classes and modules
- The physical hardware layout of the device
= E.g. inline attachments in Gmail vs a legacy "Update" step that lost files.

? Which is a characteristic of a good representation?
+ It makes relationships explicit and leaves out extra detail
- It shows every internal detail so nothing is hidden
- It hides all constraints so users feel free
- It separates objects from the actions done on them
= It also keeps objects and actions together and exposes constraints.

? Pouring orange juice into your coffee instead of the glass is a:
+ Action-based slip (right action, wrong object)
- Knowledge-based mistake (wrong mental model)
- Rule-based mistake (wrong rule applied)
- Memory-lapse mistake (forgot the goal)
= The plan was right; the execution went wrong.

? Forgetting to collect your card after an ATM withdrawal is a:
+ Memory-lapse slip
- Rule-based mistake
- Knowledge-based mistake
- Action slip
= One step was forgotten.

? Correctly assessing the situation but applying the wrong rule is a:
+ Rule-based mistake
- Knowledge-based mistake
- Action slip
- Memory-lapse slip
= A knowledge-based mistake is a wrong assessment through lack of knowledge.

? Learned helplessness happens when:
+ Repeated failures make the user give up trying
- The user practises until they become an expert
- The designer forgets who the users are
- The system is too simple to be interesting
= Avoid it with forgiving designs and support for different learners.

? Expert blindness is when:
+ Designers skip steps that seem "obvious" to them but not to novices
- Expert users can no longer see small text and icons on the screen
- Users refuse to learn anything new once they are experts
- The interface uses no colour, so experts miss warnings
= The fix is to test with novices.

? Which design principles most help users form accurate mental models?
+ Consistency, affordance and mapping
- Bright colours and sound effects
- Long, detailed user manuals
- Random layouts on each screen
= They make the system's behaviour predictable.
